++diff --git a/s6patch霍尔/0005-misc-Match-the-vendor-elevator-driver-s-timings-stat.patch b/s6patch霍尔/0005-misc-Match-the-vendor-elevator-driver-s-timings-stat.patch
++new file mode 100644
++index 000000000..e009f428f
++--- /dev/null
+++++ b/s6patch霍尔/0005-misc-Match-the-vendor-elevator-driver-s-timings-stat.patch
++@@ -0,0 +1,1501 @@
+++From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+++From: Zhiyu722 <Zhiyu722@users.noreply.github.com>
+++Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 23:06:59 +0800
+++Subject: [PATCH] misc: Match the vendor elevator driver's timings, states and
+++ key events
+++
+++A deeper disassembly of the factory gpio_pwm driver corrected several things in
+++the first version of this rewrite:
+++
+++ - The move durations come from all_time (module parameter mhall_control7, 2839
+++   by default) and not from fixed constants: a full travel takes all_time, the
+++   69.6% position takes all_time * 696 / 1000, and moving between the two takes
+++   the difference.  Every duration is then scaled by 6 / 10, the ratio between
+++   the nominal 19200 Hz and the 32000 Hz a move actually runs at, before it is
+++   handed to the hrtimer; durations above 65536 run a 50 ms preliminary stage
+++   first and then the remainder at 41600 Hz scaled by 6 / 13.
+++ - vib_pwm_camera_state has more states than 0, 1 and 2: 4 and 5 mean that a
+++   move is running, 6 means the anti-pinch logic stopped the elevator and 7 is
+++   the holder position between 1 and 2.  Finishing a move must write the target
+++   into camera_state, otherwise the busy test (state & ~1) == 4 refuses every
+++   later request.
+++ - vib_pwm_enable takes 1 for up and 2 for down, and any other value only
+++   brakes the driver; it does not touch the move state or the timer.
+++ - vib_pwm_time treats 1300 as "all_time plus ten", not as a literal duration.
+++ - The show strings are part of the interface: vib_id=%d, vib_freq=%d,
+++   count=%d, enable=%d, dir=%d, time=%d all_time=%d, and clear_cali_data
+++   reports the constant "clear".
+++ - vib_pwm_abort_notify is a keyword protocol, not a number: the camera stack
+++   writes "0x27c"/"636", "0x27d"/"637", "0x27f"/"639", "0x280"/"640",
+++   "0x282"/"642", "0x283"/"643", "0x284"/"644", "666", "888", "stream on",
+++   "stream off", "username:" and "com.eebbk.askhomework", and each one triggers
+++   its own elevator command, key event or restart of the current mode.
+++ - The driver also registers the input device h110-vib-input (phys
+++   h110-vib/input0, bus type 0x19) and injects keys 635 to 646, one press and
+++   one release per event.  The camera stack watches those, so leaving them out
+++   would leave the elevator mechanically working but invisible to userspace.
+++ - The gpio sequence, the clock handling and the state machine run under the
+++   driver lock, and the timer callback no longer tries to cancel its own timer,
+++   which would spin forever in hrtimer_cancel().
+++ - The module parameters mhall_control_up, _down and 2 to 7 are provided with
+++   the factory names, permissions and default values.
+++ - The private data is zeroed, where the vendor left enable, target_state,
+++   holder_mode, all_count and notify_str uninitialised.
+++
+++The two reverse engineering reports that back this up are added by the next
+++commit.
+++
+++Signed-off-by: Zhiyu722 <Zhiyu722@users.noreply.github.com>
+++---
+++ drivers/misc/bbk_vib_pwm.c | 1018 ++++++++++++++++++++++++------------
+++ 1 file changed, 692 insertions(+), 326 deletions(-)
+++
+++diff --git a/drivers/misc/bbk_vib_pwm.c b/drivers/misc/bbk_vib_pwm.c
+++index 89c155862..50746c0bc 100755
+++--- a/drivers/misc/bbk_vib_pwm.c
++++++ b/drivers/misc/bbk_vib_pwm.c
+++@@ -4,72 +4,84 @@
+++  * The front camera of this device sits on a motorised elevator: it is pushed
+++  * out of the body when the camera application starts and retracted again
+++  * afterwards.  The controller driver is called vib_pwm in the factory kernel
+++- * and its source is not part of this tree, so the device tree bindings, the
+++- * sysfs interface and the motor timing were recovered from the factory boot
+++- * image and its device tree.
++++ * (its source file is drivers/input/hall/vib_pwm/gpio_pwm.c and its module
++++ * name is gpio_pwm) and it is built in, so its sources are not part of this
++++ * tree.  The device tree binding, the sysfs interface, the state machine and
++++ * the motor timing below were recovered from the factory boot image, whose
++++ * kallsyms still carries every symbol name, and from the factory device tree.
+++  *
+++- * Hardware (from the factory device tree, node soc:bbk_vib_pwm)
+++- *   compatible = "bbk,vib_pwm_control"
+++- *   boost-gpio  GPIO24   driver supply boost converter, high while moving
+++- *   enable-gpio GPIO23   driver enable, pulled low while moving
+++- *   sleep-gpio  GPIO29   driver nSLEEP, high while moving
+++- *   dir-gpio    GPIO26   0 moves the elevator up, 1 moves it down
+++- *   id-gpio     GPIO25   elevator module id strap, read through a pull-up
++++ * Hardware, from the node soc:bbk_vib_pwm (compatible "bbk,vib_pwm_control")
++++ *   boost-gpio  gpio24  driver supply boost, one while moving, zero idle
++++ *   enable-gpio gpio23  driver enable, active low: zero while moving
++++ *   sleep-gpio  gpio29  driver nSLEEP, one while moving, zero idle
++++ *   dir-gpio    gpio26  zero moves the elevator up, one moves it down
++++ *   id-gpio     gpio25  elevator module id strap, read through a pull-up
+++  *   clocks = <&gcc GCC_GP2_CLK>, clock-names = "gp2_clk"
+++  *
+++- * There is no pwm controller: the drive waveform is the general purpose clock
+++- * gcc_gp2_clk, which the "vib_pwm_active" pinctrl state routes out on gpio21,
+++- * so the chopper frequency is simply the clock rate.  gcc_gp2_clk_src is a
+++- * fractional (mnd) rcg, so a 32 kHz rate is synthesizable even though the
+++- * preset table only lists 19.2, 25, 50, 100 and 200 MHz.
++++ * There is no pwm controller in the binding: the drive waveform is the general
++++ * purpose clock gcc_gp2_clk, which the "vib_pwm_active" pinctrl state routes
++++ * out on gpio21, so the chopper frequency is simply the clock rate handed to
++++ * clk_set_rate.  GCC_GP2_CLK is clock id 36 in qcom,gcc-sdmmagpie.h and
++++ * gcc_gp2_clk_src is a fractional (mnd) rcg, so the 19200, 32000, 41600 and
++++ * 4800 Hz the vendor uses are all synthesizable even though the preset table
++++ * only lists 19.2, 25, 50, 100 and 200 MHz.
+++  *
+++- * Recovered from the factory binary (symbols are still in its kallsyms)
+++- *   vib_pwm_probe, vib_pwm_set_camera, vib_pwm_set_camera_state,
+++- *   vib_pwm_state_move_sate, vib_pwm_camera_state_show/store, the fifteen
+++- *   dev_attr_vib_pwm_* attributes, get_camera_state, cancel_vib_hrtimer,
+++- *   set_vib_all_time, set_vib_up_down_count, is_top_or_bottom,
+++- *   computer_distance, vib_is_in_cali, vib_is_move.
++++ * Camera positions.  The vendor measures the travel on a scale that runs from
++++ * 0 (retracted) to 15950 (fully extended), with anchors at 3828 (24%),
++++ * 7656 (48%) and 11101 (69.6%), and it exposes these values through
